---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "../lib/config.ts";
const site = Astro.site!.origin;
const jsonConfig = JSON.stringify({
  mcpServers: {
    opendevicedb: { command: "npx", args: ["-y", "@opendevicedb/mcp"], env: { ODB_API_URL: site } },
  },
}, null, 2);
---
<Base title="For AI agents" description="Install the OpenDeviceDB MCP server so your agent checks the directory before reverse engineering a device, claims it, and publishes its findings.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>For AI agents</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Reverse engineering with an agent is cheap; doing it twice is waste. Give your agent the
      OpenDeviceDB MCP server and it will check the directory before it starts, claim the device
      while it works, and publish what it found when it is done.
    </p>

    <h2 id="install">Install the MCP server</h2>
    <p><strong>Claude Code</strong>, with the <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@opendevicedb/mcp">published package</a> (read-only, queries this website):</p>
    <pre><code>claude mcp add opendevicedb -- npx -y @opendevicedb/mcp</code></pre>

    <p><strong>From a checkout</strong> (works today, and is the mode that can write claims and findings):</p>
    <pre><code>git clone {REPO_URL}
cd opendevicedb &amp;&amp; npm install
claude mcp add opendevicedb -e ODB_REPO="$PWD" -- node "$PWD/packages/mcp/src/server.ts"</code></pre>
    <p class="small muted">Node 22.18+ runs the TypeScript sources directly. <code>npm run mcp</code> starts the same server on stdio.</p>

    <h3 id="remote">Remote, read-only mode</h3>
    <p>
      Without a checkout, point the server at this website's static API with <code>ODB_API_URL</code>.
      Lookups and searches work; claims and publications need a checkout (<code>ODB_REPO</code>), because
      writes go through a pull request.
    </p>
    <pre><code>claude mcp add opendevicedb -e ODB_API_URL={site} -- npx -y @opendevicedb/mcp</code></pre>
    <p>Other MCP clients (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Codex…) take the same server in their JSON config:</p>
    <pre><code>{jsonConfig}</code></pre>
    <p class="small muted">
      If both are set, <code>ODB_REPO</code> wins. With neither, the server reads the checkout it runs from.
    </p>

    <h2 id="workflow">The workflow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Look up</strong> — <code>lookup_device</code> with every identifier you have (FCC ID, USB <code>vid:pid</code>, BLE name, Zigbee model, then vendor + model). It says what exists, who is working on it, and what to do next. <code>search_devices</code> explores by name.</li>
      <li><strong>Claim</strong> — nothing usable exists? <code>claim_device</code> with who you are, a contact, the scope ("BLE control protocol") and a duration (30 days by default, 90 max). Others now see the device as <em>in progress</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Work</strong> — capture, decode, write the SDK or firmware. Host the code and docs in your own repository under an open license.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish</strong> — <code>publish_findings</code> merges interfaces (with an honest <code>confidence</code>), projects, identifiers and verification into the record, validates it against the schema, and refuses content the <a href="/policy/">policy</a> excludes. Flag <code>provenance.ai_assisted</code> truthfully. Publish partial results too: a documented telemetry channel already saves the next person a week.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull request</strong> — the tool prints the exact <code>git</code> / <code>gh pr create</code> commands. A human reviews, CI validates, and the record goes live on the next build.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="trust">What agents should know</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Records from imported databases are slim: a name, a status and a link back. Follow the link.</li>
      <li>AI output is not trusted by default. "Verified on hardware" means a replayable test (<code>hardware-replay</code>) was run on a real device with a stated firmware version — nothing less.</li>
      <li>Claims expire. An expired claim is ignored; a live one comes with a contact — use it before duplicating the work.</li>
      <li>Never publish firmware images, vendor code, keys, or anything defeating Remote ID, geofencing, radio limits or other safety functions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="api">Static API</h2>
    <p>The same data, as plain JSON (what the server reads in remote mode):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/api/index.json"><code>/api/index.json</code></a> — every device: <code>id, vendor, model, aliases, category, status, sources, identifiers, claims, verified, updated</code></li>
      <li><code>/api/devices/&lt;vendor&gt;/&lt;model&gt;.json</code> — the full merged record, e.g. <a href="/api/devices/ryze/tello.json"><code>/api/devices/ryze/tello.json</code></a></li>
      <li><a href="/api/sources.json"><code>/api/sources.json</code></a> — imported sources with licenses, snapshot dates and counts</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="small muted">The agent skill file shipped with the server (<code>packages/mcp/SKILL.md</code>) is CC0, so other tools can embed it.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
